{
    "title": "Airworthiness Directives; Airbus Helicopters",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 08, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-08/pdf/2025-22250.pdf",
    "summary": "The U.S. aviation regulator has issued two new safety rules for certain Airbus helicopters to reduce the risk of in\u2011flight failures. One targets wear and tear in the main gearbox mounting hardware, and the other tightens inspections and procedures for fuel filter bowls after new crack findings.\n\nFor Airbus Helicopters AS332 models (C, C1, L, L1), the first rule aims to prevent fatigue failures in the fittings and bolts that secure the main gearbox suspension bars. It requires operators to follow updated inspection and replacement limits, with some U.S.-specific exceptions to timing and paperwork. The goal is straightforward: keep these parts from staying in service past their safe life so a suspension bar can\u2019t detach and jeopardize control of the helicopter.\n\nThe second rule replaces a 2024 action and focuses on the fuel filter bowls on AS332 models (C, C1, L, L1, L2) and the EC225LP, if they use certain filter part numbers and were certified before February 15, 2024. After new cracks were found on the inside of the bowls\u2014likely from over\u2011tightening\u2014this update adds an inner\u2011surface inspection and revises the tightening torque. It also narrows which helicopters are covered. The rule takes effect January 12, 2026, and prohibits special flight permits, meaning aircraft that don\u2019t meet the requirements can\u2019t be flown just to get to a repair facility. The regulator estimates inspections take about two labor hours per helicopter, while a filter replacement could cost several thousand dollars in parts and labor.\n\nWho benefits? Pilots, passengers, and maintenance teams gain from clearer limits on critical hardware and more precise inspection steps that reduce the chance of sudden failures. Standardized procedures help catch problems earlier and support safer, more predictable operations.\n\nWho might be negatively affected? Operators will face added downtime, scheduling pressures, and potential costs for inspections and part replacements\u2014especially smaller fleets or older aircraft with affected parts. Some may also feel the pinch from parts availability and the no\u2011ferry\u2011flight restriction in the fuel filter rule.\n\nOverall, these updates align U.S. actions with the latest manufacturer testing and European guidance, aiming to address known risks without adding unnecessary complexity. Operators may want to plan inspections early, line up parts, and coordinate with their maintenance providers to minimize disruption while meeting the new requirements.",
